## Workbook — document and program

A workbook is one `.html` file that carries its *entire filesystem* inside it, compressed. That filesystem holds the interface (markup/styles/script), the data it carries, its own source (so it can be unbundled), and — when it computes — the WebAssembly modules run by an embedded kernel or by the runtime's wasmtime. The filesystem rides as a single base64 block, `<script type`"application/zip" id="wb-bundle">…</script>= — a deflate-zip, so the served page unpacks itself in any browser with no dependency (`DecompressionStream('deflate-raw')` is native; zstd/xz are not). It does not need a server to simply be used; it is opened, not hosted. (The separate `.wbundle` is legacy back-compat; new artifacts are the self-contained `.html`.)

Every buildable workbook exists in three projections — keep them straight:

- *runnable* — what executes and ships: compiled `.wasm` + built JS/CSS,
embedded in the HTML.
- *source* — what you read, diff, and edit: the input files (components,
crates, org documents, configs) on the git rail.
- *archive* — both together.

The law: never hand-edit the runnable artifact, and never let raw build inputs leak into it. If you are editing compiled HTML, you are on the wrong projection — unbundle, edit source, rebuild.

## Bundle ⇄ unbundle

- *Bundle* (source → artifact): the build compiles each component with its
lane — compute sources → `.wasm`; UI projects build with their own declared build (the framework is not special-cased); org documents tangle their `:workflow:` / `:component:` headlines into the executable plan. A component whose toolchain is unavailable is reported *unbuilt with a reason* — the platform never fakes an artifact. Treat `unbuilt` as a to-do.
- *Unbundle* (artifact → source): extracts the `wb-bundle` block and unpacks
the deflate-zip back into an editable filesystem tree. This is the intended editing loop for any workbook you receive as a file: unbundle → edit source → rebuild. Neither direction is lossy; round-tripping is byte-exact.

Bundle/unbundle is the workbook's native edit-and-compile lifecycle, not a one-off export: you always edit *source* (never the compiled `.wasm`), and re-bundling replaces the embedded payload cleanly.

## Runtime — one server, many apps

The runtime is one Elixir/BEAM engine that serves workbooks, hosts agents, and executes all compute as WebAssembly on wasmtime. It is *not* a per-app server — one runtime propagates many apps and agents and runs their concurrent processes on a single machine. As an agent you are one process on it; the workdir handed to you is a tenant git repo on its data volume.

## Toolkit — capability + skill

A toolkit is how anything gains a capability: a directory with a `manifest.org` (the `:toolkit:` front door) and `skills/*.org` recipes you read progressively. It is not a plugin API and not a tool-registry entry — to you it is commands on PATH plus org files. Driving toolkits is the `toolkits` skill.

## Workflow — the org outline IS the spec

There is no DSL. A native org outline is the orchestration:

- TODO keywords are task *states*; a `#+TODO:` line overrides the default set.
- Heading nesting is sub-workflows (parent) vs. units of work (leaf).
- `:PROPERTIES:` drawers carry edges and gates (`:ORDERED:`, `:BLOCKER:`,
`:NEXT:`, acceptance `:DONE-WHEN:`).
- In a component DAG, `:component:` children are tasks and `:out`→`:in`
header args are edges; a `#+begin_src agent` component runs an agent loop.

Specs are *loaded artifacts* — re-read each tick, so they hot-swap live. Property keys are upcased on parse (`:next:` ≡ `:NEXT:`). Details: `workflows`.

## Dock — the capability membrane

The Dock is the boundary between a workbook's (or toolkit's) WASM and the host. Filesystem, network, and time are *host-brokered imports* granted by policy — never ambient, never inherited. Toolkit instances are isolated WASM instances on one shared engine; nesting is host-brokered (a flat forest, no wasm-in-wasm), and capabilities flow down by explicit grant. This is why your `fetch` works only when network is granted, and why files go through `vfs_read` / `vfs_write` rather than raw OS paths.

## HOST vs LOADED — the safety boundary

- *HOST* — the engine code, fixed at deploy time; changes only via a new
runtime image (an operator action, outside your reach).
- *LOADED* — workbooks, toolkits, agent defs, workflow/lifecycle specs,
boards; these hot-swap on the live engine.

You edit LOADED, never HOST. Self-modifying behavior is safe precisely because it touches loaded artifacts only.